;; =============================================================================
;; Spec: Phase-LLM stream-idle as a `:phase/timeout` terminal verdict
;; =============================================================================
;;
;; Dogfood evidence (2026-06-05, review-redirect-convergence run): workflow
;; `adhoc-944448986` walked explore → plan (8.7m) → implement task 1 (8.7m)
;; → verify task 1 (12.7m), all clean. Review died at task 1 because the
;; reviewer LLM stream-idle'd at 360s. The framework promoted the adaptive
;; timeout text into `:review/blocking-issues`, set `:review/decision :rejected`,
;; the `:review-approved` gate correctly rejected, `:phase/verdict :exhausted`
;; fired, the DAG cascade-failed task 2.
;;
;; The reviewer already has a `timeout-only-review?` predicate (reviewer/
;; llm_response.clj) + a `:reviewer/backend-timeout` error path. In this
;; dogfood it did NOT fire — likely because the framework's synthesized
;; "rejection-with-timeout-text" artifact didn't match the strict shape the
;; predicate requires (or the call path bypassed it). Either way, planner /
;; implementer / verifier have no analogous wiring, so any phase agent's
;; stream-idle will keep cascade-failing.
;;
;; This is adjacent to but distinct from the network-resilience stack
;; (#1051/#1053/#1054). Network-drop = TCP/TLS reachability lost; stream-idle
;; = provider connected but stopped producing tokens for N seconds.
;; Recovery shape is similar (terminal verdict + checkpoint resume) — that's
;; the design target here.

{:spec/title "Phase-LLM stream-idle should produce a `:phase/timeout` terminal verdict, not `:rejected`"

 :spec/description
 "Treat a phase-LLM stream-idle timeout as an INFRA failure, not a code-review
  / planner / implementer rejection. Three changes, each phase-shaped:

  (a) DETECTION. Add a uniform `stream-idle-in-result?` predicate (mirror of
  `network-drop-in-result?` in phase_software_factory/implement.clj). Reads
  `[:error :data :timeout :type]` and `[:timeout :type]` for `:stream-idle`,
  plus the message-channel `type: stream-idle` marker the same way the
  network-drop classifier does. Audit the reviewer's existing
  `timeout-only-review?` (agent/reviewer/llm_response.clj) for shape drift
  against what the framework actually produces when the LLM call times out
  with no parsed response — the 2026-06-05 dogfood proves the predicate did
  NOT fire on the production path.

  (b) VERDICT. Introduce `:phase/timeout` as a TERMINAL verdict in the
  per-phase compute-verdict pipelines (implement.clj's `network-drop-in-result?`
  arm has the right shape — reuse, don't fork). `:phase/timeout` ranks above
  `:repair-requested` so a transient infra timeout never burns a real
  redirect/repair cycle, and below `:stagnated?` / `:needs-decomposition?`
  so existing terminal classifications keep priority. Reviewer's
  `:reviewer/backend-timeout` path is the prototype — generalize and apply
  to planner, implementer, verifier, reviewer uniformly.

  (c) COMPOSE WITH AUTO-RESUME. `:phase/timeout` must compose with the PR-C
  workflow-checkpoint resume path (#1054). On terminal `:phase/timeout` the
  DAG SHOULD retry the phase from the last persisted checkpoint instead of
  cascade-failing the task. The retry semantics mirror `:network-drop`:
  retry once with the same checkpoint; only after a configured retry budget
  is exhausted does the task fail terminally.

  Non-goals: changing what counts as `:stream-idle` (the adaptive-timeout
  thresholds remain; this spec only changes what HAPPENS when one fires).
  Tuning the reviewer's 360s threshold separately."

 :spec/intent
 {:type :feature
  :scope ["components/phase-software-factory/src"
          "components/agent/src/ai/miniforge/agent/reviewer"
          "components/agent/src/ai/miniforge/agent/result_boundary.clj"
          "components/workflow/src"
          "components/phase/resources/config"]}

 :spec/constraints
 ["The detection predicate MUST mirror `network-drop-in-result?` in
   components/phase-software-factory/src/ai/miniforge/phase_software_factory/implement.clj
   (path priority [:error :data :timeout :type] → [:timeout :type] → message-channel marker)
   so the two timeout families stay symmetrically shaped"
  "`:phase/timeout` is a NEW terminal verdict distinct from `:rejected`,
   `:exhausted`, `:needs-decomposition`, `:network-drop`. It conveys 'infra
   timeout in the phase-LLM call' — actionable as a retry signal, not as a
   defect"
  "The existing reviewer `timeout-only-review?` + `timeout-only-error-result`
   path must be audited against the 2026-06-05 dogfood evidence — fix the
   shape mismatch so it actually fires on a real reviewer stream-idle, then
   factor the audit's lessons into the cross-phase pattern"
  "Auto-resume budget is bounded: default 1 retry per phase-LLM call (config-
   driven, EDN-owned per rule 007); after the budget exhausts the task
   terminal-fails so an upstream infra outage cannot loop indefinitely"
  "No regression: a planner/reviewer/implementer that produces a real verdict
   (approved/rejected/changes-requested/etc.) still flows through the existing
   compute-verdict pipeline unchanged. Only the stream-idle terminal case
   takes the new path"
  "Telemetry: a per-phase `:phase/stream-idle-detected` event + the resulting
   verdict (`:phase/timeout` vs `:rejected` if detection mis-fires) must be
   emitted so dogfood post-mortems can verify the new path"]

 :spec/acceptance-criteria
 ["A reviewer LLM that stream-idles at 360s (the 2026-06-05 dogfood shape)
   produces a `:phase/timeout` terminal verdict and the framework schedules
   a single retry from the last persisted checkpoint — it does NOT cascade-
   fail the DAG task, and it does NOT promote the timeout text into
   `:review/blocking-issues`"
  "A planner LLM that stream-idles produces the same `:phase/timeout` verdict
   (no planner-shaped regression — symmetry across phase agents)"
  "An implementer LLM that stream-idles produces `:phase/timeout`,
   composing with the existing `network-drop-in-result?` arm in
   `leave-implement` (the two arms sit side by side and never both fire on
   the same result)"
  "When the configured retry budget is exhausted (default 1), the phase
   fails terminally with the `:phase/timeout` verdict preserved — the DAG
   knows to mark the task failed AND distinguishes it from a real-defect
   `:rejected` in the events stream"
  "The reviewer's existing `timeout-only-review?` predicate either fires
   correctly on a real reviewer stream-idle (audit + fix), or is replaced
   by the new cross-phase detection — no redundant overlap"
  "A normal review/plan/implement that does NOT stream-idle continues to
   reach release unchanged (no regression on the happy path)"
  "Re-running the dogfood spec that previously died at the reviewer stream-
   idle (`work/review-redirect-convergence.spec.edn` or any equivalent)
   now retries the phase once, and if the retry succeeds the DAG task
   completes — it does not cascade-fail on a single transient timeout"
  "A new `:phase/stream-idle-detected` event is emitted on every stream-idle
   detection (separate from the resulting verdict), so post-mortems can
   measure stream-idle frequency per backend / per phase"]

 :spec/priority
 {:tier :blocker
  :axes #{:dogfood-enabler :correctness :token-conservation}
  :theme :dogfood-resilience
  :rationale "Every phase-LLM call has stream-idle exposure. Without this, any reviewer / planner / implementer that hits a slow provider response cascade-fails the DAG and burns the parent workflow. The 2026-06-05 dogfood (`adhoc-944448986`) lost a $2.95 / 40m run to exactly this on the first review. Composes with the just-shipped network-resilience stack — same recovery shape, different trigger."
  :blocks []
  :blocked-by []}

 :spec/workflow-type :canonical-sdlc}
